        "math_academy_id": "q-107495",
        "problem": "Find the principal $20$th root of unity.",
        "worked_solution": "Let $z$ be a $20$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\n{z}^{20}=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}\u2061(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{20}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,\u27f9\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/20}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to ${e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/10}.$",
